Plan generated: {'plan': '# Step-by-Step Plan: Forecast USD/CNY Exchange Rate for Next Trading Day\n\n## Phase 1: Define Scope & Requirements\n1. **Clarify the target**: Confirm the specific pair (USD/CNY onshore vs. USD/CNH offshore), the exact "next trading day" (accounting for weekends/holidays in both US and China markets), and the forecast type (point estimate, range, or probabilistic).\n2. **Set success criteria**: Define acceptable error metrics (e.g., MAE, RMSE, directional accuracy) and the confidence level required.\n3. **Identify constraints**: Note any latency, data licensing, or regulatory constraints.\n\n## Phase 2: Data Collection\n4. **Historical price data**: Gather daily (and intraday if available) USD/CNY rates — at minimum 2–5 years for seasonality and regime coverage.\n5. **Macroeconomic indicators**:\n   - US: Fed funds rate, CPI, NFP, DXY, Treasury yields\n   - China: PBoC midpoint fix, LPR, PMI, trade balance, FX reserves\n6. **Policy & intervention signals**: PBoC daily fixing, counter-cyclical factor, capital controls news.\n7. **Market microstructure**: Order flow, bid-ask spreads, offshore-onshore spread (CNH-CNY).\n8. **Event calendar**: Upcoming data releases, central bank meetings, geopolitical events.\n\n## Phase 3: Data Preparation\n9. **Clean & align**: Handle missing values, align timestamps across time zones, adjust for holidays.\n10. **Feature engineering**: Compute returns, volatility (realized/GARCH), moving averages, momentum, rate differentials, and sentiment proxies.\n11. **Stationarity checks**: Apply ADF/KPSS tests; difference or transform as needed.\n12. **Train/validation/test split**: Use time-series-aware splitting (no shuffling) to avoid look-ahead bias.\n\n## Phase 4: Model Selection & Development\n13. **Baseline models**: Random walk (naïve), ARIMA, exponential smoothing.\n14. **Econometric models**: Vector autoregression (VAR), error correction model (ECM) capturing cointegration with rate differentials.\n15. **ML models**: Gradient boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM), random forest with lagged features.\n16. **Deep learning (optional)**: LSTM/Transformer if sufficient data and compute.\n17. **Ensemble**: Combine models via weighted averaging or stacking.\n\n## Phase 5: Validation & Backtesting\n18. **Walk-forward validation**: Retrain on rolling windows to simulate real forecasting.\n19. **Evaluate metrics**: MAE, RMSE, MAPE, directional accuracy, Sharpe of a simple trading rule.\n20. **Stress tests**: Evaluate performance during high-volatility regimes (e.g., 2015 devaluation, 2022 Fed hikes).\n21. **Compare vs. baseline**: Only proceed if model beats random walk meaningfully.\n\n## Phase 6: Generate the Forecast\n22. **Retrain on full data**: Fit the selected model(s) on all available history.\n23. **Incorporate latest inputs**: Feed in the most recent fixing, closes, and any overnight news.\n24. **Produce output**:\n    - Point forecast for next trading day\n    - Prediction interval (e.g., 80% / 95%)\n    - Directional bias (appreciation/depreciation)\n25. **Sanity check**: Compare against forward points, analyst consensus, and implied vol.\n\n## Phase 7: Reporting & Delivery\n26. **Document assumptions**: List data sources, model version, and known limitations.\n27. **Present results**: Forecast value, confidence band, key drivers, and risk factors.\n28. **Add disclaimer**: Note that FX forecasts are inherently uncertain and not financial advice.\n\n## Phase 8: Monitoring & Iteration\n29. **Track live performance**: Log actual vs. predicted; monitor drift.\n30. **Retrain schedule**: Set cadence (daily/weekly) and triggers (regime shifts, error spikes).\n31. **Feedback loop**: Update features, models, and assumptions based on realized outcomes.\n\n---\n\n**Key Risks to Flag Upfront:**\n- PBoC intervention can override market-driven signals.\n- Geopolitical shocks create non-stationary behavior.\n- Data latency between onshore close and next-day open.\n- Overfitting risk with high-dimensional ML models on limited FX history.'}
